{
  "dom_id": "step-e1317",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
